```cs
Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0, "index of x after insertion is more than or equal to 0");
```
As you can see, the Debug.Assert method is used to check the condition inside the brackets and if that condition is false, it will output the string provided as the second argument. We expect New_arrListIndexOfX to be greater or equal to 0, so we add a message to display if this condition doesn't hold true.--------------------This is the output in C# which generates the Debug.Assert statements corresponding to the comments that start with "@@@"

```cs

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_InsertContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x, int index)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index <= arrList.Count && index >= 0);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_index = index;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            arrList.Insert(index, x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_index", Old_index);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_x", Old_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x", New_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_contains_x", Old_arrListContainsX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);

            // Make sure index of x after insertion is more than or equal to 0
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0);
        }
    }
}

```--------------------The given code is in C# and checks assumptions about the `ArrayList` type. However, as no asserts were specified in the code, it's hard to provide a direct translation. However, here is an example how you might use the `Debug.Assert` from .NET to do similar checks. 

```cs
public class ArrayListContract 
{
    public void TestInsert(ArrayList arrList, int x, int index)
    {
        Assert.IsTrue(index <= arrList.Count && index >= 0, "Index is out of bounds");

        // remember the precondition
        arrList.Insert(index, x);

        // now validate each condition in the method that's been tested, using Debug.Assert
        Debug.Assert(arrList.Count == Old_arrListCount + 1, "Count is not increased after the insertion");
        Debug.Assert(arrList.IndexOf(x) >= index, "Index of x is not properly computed");
        Debug.Assert(arrList.LastIndexOf(x) == New_arrListLastIndexOfX || New_arrListLastIndexOfX == -1, "LastIndexOf x is not properly computed");
        Debug.Assert(arrList.Contains(x), "ArrayList does not contain the value after insertion");
    }
}
```

The `Debug.Assert` calls are just like `Assert.IsTrue`, but only generate a message when a condition is not fulfilled.

Please note that `Debug.Assert` throws an `AssertionException` when the condition is not met which may or may not be what you want based on the context. That, and the code will only get executed in a DEBUG build. So, if you want your checks to happen regardless of build type, use `Assert.IsTrue`.--------------------Here is the C# code that corresponds to the comments in the code:

